The "mount" symlink in this directory (/usr/lib/live/mount -> /run/live,
reached as /lib/live/mount via the merged-/usr symlink) restores a path that
live-config still depends on but live-boot no longer provides.

Background:

  live-boot mounts the boot medium at /run/live/medium. Up to and including
  buster, live-boot additionally bind-mounted that tree onto the historical
  path so old consumers kept working -- see 9990-main.sh in buster's
  live-boot:

      # avoid breaking existing user scripts that rely on the old path
      mkdir -p ${rootmnt}/lib/live/mount
      mount --rbind /run/live ${rootmnt}/lib/live/mount
      # XXX: to be removed before the bullseye release

  That shim was duly removed. live-config, however, was never updated: as of
  11.0.5 it still looks *only* under /lib/live/mount/medium --

      usr/lib/live/init-config.sh   (config.conf, config.conf.d/*.conf)
      usr/lib/live/config/0010-debconf   (config-preseed/*)
      usr/lib/live/config/9990-hooks     (config-hooks/*)

The consequence is that everything the ISO ships on the medium side under
/live (config.conf, config.conf.d/user-setup.conf, ...) is silently ignored.
For this image that meant LIVE_USERNAME never got set, so live-config's
user-setup component created no login account and
live-config-getty-generator's `[ -n "${LIVE_USERNAME}" ]` guard skipped
creating the tty autologin drop-ins -- the ISO booted to a login prompt
instead of straight into vbash.

Restoring the path lets the stock live-config mechanism work as designed,
rather than reimplementing autologin ourselves.
